    "answer": {
      "text": "Not every new moon produces a total solar eclipse because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5 degrees relative to Earth's orbit, so most new moons occur when the Moon passes slightly above or below the Sun from our perspective. Totality only happens when a new moon coincides with the Moon being near one of its orbital nodes—the points where its path crosses Earth's orbital plane—allowing its shadow to fall directly on Earth.\n\n**Remaining uncertainty:** None. The orbital mechanics and geometry of eclipses are well-established and do not require new verification. The provided NASA source confirms these facts without ambiguity.",
      "sha256": "1bcae391d703985cf18482766d6b2178ddc32271be5cb18c9d0863a50272a9b9"
